# OpenDiving API configuration.
#
#   cp src/.env.example src/.env
#
# Then generate a SECRET_KEY and change POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Everything uncommented below is
# enough to bring the stack up with `docker compose up`; the commented-out settings show
# their built-in defaults and only need touching if you want something else.
# `src/app/core/config.py` is the full, authoritative list.
#
# Two of the settings here refuse to start the app rather than run on a value copied from
# a template - SECRET_KEY below, and ADMIN_PASSWORD if you enable the admin panel in
# production. Anything commented out is commented out on purpose; uncommenting a block is
# a decision, not a formality.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD is the exception, and worth knowing about: docker compose feeds this
# same file to the `db` container, so the POSTGRES_* values below are both the app's
# connection settings and the credentials Postgres initializes itself with. `change-me`
# therefore is a live password the moment you run `docker compose up` - nothing rejects
# it, because rejecting it would stop the local stack coming up at all. It is survivable
# locally only because compose binds Postgres to 127.0.0.1; change it before this
# database is reachable from anywhere else.

# -------- Application (OpenAPI metadata shown at /docs) --------
APP_NAME="OpenDiving"
APP_DESCRIPTION="Backend API for OpenDiving."
# LICENSE="AGPL-3.0"
# Who maintains this API. Metadata only - this is NOT where the contact form delivers
# to; that's CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL near the bottom.
# CONTACT_NAME="Your Name"
# CONTACT_EMAIL="you@example.com"

# -------- Database --------
POSTGRES_USER="postgres"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD="change-me"
# "db" is the compose service name. Use "localhost" when running the API outside docker.
POSTGRES_SERVER="db"
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_DB="opendive"
# The API runs `alembic upgrade head` on startup, so an upgrade is `docker compose pull &&
# docker compose up -d` and nothing else. Turn it off only if you would rather run the
# migrations yourself (`docker compose run --rm api alembic upgrade head`) - the app then
# boots against whatever schema it finds, and a version behind will fail on the first query
# that needs the new column.
# MIGRATE_ON_START=true

# -------- Security --------
# Signs access/refresh/onboarding tokens, so anyone holding this value can mint one for
# any account. Generate your own - the placeholder below is published in this repository
# and the app REFUSES TO START on it, in every environment:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it later signs every existing session out.
SECRET_KEY="change-me-openssl-rand-hex-32"
# ALGORITHM="HS256"
# ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7
# ONBOARDING_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# How long a parsed dive-computer file stays attachable to a dive. Long on purpose - a
# diver may import a file, then spend an evening filling in sites and gear before saving.
# DIVE_FILE_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=1440
#
# The refresh cookie is Secure, so it is only sent back over HTTPS. Set this to false ONLY
# for an instance served over plain HTTP - a LAN address with no certificate - where the
# browser otherwise drops the cookie silently and every reload signs the user out.
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# -------- Logging --------
# DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL, applied to the API and the worker alike. An
# unrecognised value fails at startup rather than at the first log line.
# LOG_LEVEL="INFO"

# -------- Redis (cache + arq queue) --------
REDIS_CACHE_HOST="redis"
REDIS_CACHE_PORT=6379
REDIS_QUEUE_HOST="redis"
REDIS_QUEUE_PORT=6379
# One password for both pools. The bundled `redis` container needs none; a managed Redis
# or one running with `requirepass` does.
# REDIS_PASSWORD=""
# CLIENT_CACHE_MAX_AGE=60

# -------- First superuser --------
# Created by `python -m src.scripts.create_first_superuser`, and the login for the
# CRUDAdmin interface at /admin (which is off by default - see the bottom of this file).
# ADMIN_PASSWORD has no default. Leave it unset and no admin account is created at all;
# set it and you get one with full create/update/delete over every model. On
# ENVIRONMENT="production" with CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED=true, startup fails unless this is a
# real password.
#
# ADMIN_EMAIL has no default either, and ships commented out for the same reason
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS does: sign-in is passwordless and keyed on the address, so an
# address left over from this template is a superuser account whose sign-in link goes
# somewhere you don't read. Unset, the script creates nothing and says so.
ADMIN_NAME="Your Name"
# ADMIN_EMAIL="you@example.com"
# ADMIN_USERNAME="admin"
# ADMIN_PASSWORD="a-real-password-not-this-one"

# -------- Environment --------
# local | staging | production. Controls the interactive docs: open at /docs on "local",
# superuser-only on "staging", entirely absent on "production". Also makes SMTP_HOST
# mandatory on anything but "local": sign-in is passwordless, so an instance with no relay
# cannot let anybody in, including its first user, and it fails at startup rather than at
# that discovery. "local" is the only value that falls back to writing the sign-in link to
# the logs, because that link is a live credential and those logs are read by more than
# one person anywhere else.
ENVIRONMENT="local"

# -------- Reverse proxy --------
# Addresses or CIDR blocks of proxies in front of this app whose X-Forwarded-For header
# should be believed. Every per-IP rate limit depends on it.
#
# Leave unset when the app is reached directly (including plain `docker compose up`,
# where the API is published straight to the host) - the socket peer is then the real
# caller and the header must NOT be trusted, or anyone can forge an address per request
# and skip the limits.
#
# Set it when you put nginx/an ALB/Cloudflare in front, otherwise the opposite failure
# applies: every caller looks like the proxy, all the per-IP buckets merge into one, and
# a single bot exhausting the magic-link limit locks sign-in out for everybody. For a
# proxy on a default Docker bridge network that is the container subnet:
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS="172.16.0.0/12"

# -------- Frontend --------
# Used to build the links in outgoing email ({FRONTEND_URL}/auth/verify?token=...) and,
# just as importantly, as the single allowed CORS origin - a browser that can load the
# web app but gets CORS errors from the API is almost always this being wrong. Left at
# the default on a real deployment, every emailed sign-in link points at localhost.
#
# It is also the passkey domain - see the Passkeys section below before changing its
# hostname, which silently orphans every passkey already registered.
FRONTEND_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# -------- Email (SMTP) --------
# Leave SMTP_HOST unset for local development: nothing is sent, and the magic-link URL
# is written to the API logs instead, which is the intended local sign-in flow.
# ENVIRONMENT="local" is the only value that allows that - on anything else, staging
# included, startup fails without a relay - see above.
#
# Point SMTP_* at any relay you trust - your provider, your host's relay, or your own
# existing mail server. Don't stand up an MTA for this unless you already know why:
# sending mail is easy, getting it *accepted* (PTR, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, IP reputation, a
# port 25 your host probably blocks) is the hard part, and it's why every serious
# self-hosted app asks for relay credentials instead of bundling Postfix.
#
# SMTP_HOST="smtp.example.com"
# SMTP_PORT=587
# starttls (587, the default) | tls (465) | none (a relay on localhost or the compose
# network - nothing else). Certificates are verified in both TLS modes.
# SMTP_TLS_MODE="starttls"
# Both optional and independent: an anonymous relay needs neither. The login is only
# attempted when SMTP_USERNAME is set.
# SMTP_USERNAME="..."
# SMTP_PASSWORD="..."
#
# Resend users: SMTP_HOST="smtp.resend.com", SMTP_USERNAME="resend", and SMTP_PASSWORD
# is the API key. Mail still shows up in the Resend dashboard.
#
# Reading mail in a local inbox instead: `docker compose --profile mail up` and set
#   SMTP_HOST="mailpit"
#   SMTP_PORT=1025
#   SMTP_TLS_MODE="none"
# then open http://localhost:8025. To go back to logged links, comment SMTP_HOST out
# again - left set with no Mailpit running, sends fail against a dead host and no link
# is logged either. Any edit to this file needs `docker compose up -d --force-recreate
# api` to take effect - a plain `restart` reuses the environment the container was
# created with.

# Required as soon as SMTP_HOST is set - startup fails without it, deliberately. There
# is no sane default: it has to be an address on a domain your relay is allowed to send
# for, and a wrong one fails at delivery time, hours later, in someone's spam folder.
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS="noreply@yourdomain.example"

# -------- Contact form (POST /api/v1/contact) --------
# Where the web app's contact form delivers to. No default and none possible: it used to
# reach the upstream project's own inbox, which meant a self-hosted instance quietly
# forwarded its users' support mail to strangers who can neither see that server nor help
# them. Unset, the endpoint answers 503 and the form is simply off. Delivery also needs
# SMTP_HOST above.
# CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL="you@example.com"
# Fixed-window rate limits on that endpoint, keyed by submitted email and by client IP.
# It's unauthenticated and sends mail, so these are what keep it from being used as a relay.
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# CONTACT_FORM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=10

# -------- Geocoding (GET /api/v1/geocode/*) --------
# Turns a map pin into "Dahab, Egypt", and "blue hole dahab" into a position. Proxied
# server-side so no key ever reaches the browser and the web app's CSP needs no extra
# host. Works out of the box with no account.
#
# PRIVACY: on a cache miss, the pinned coordinate or the typed search string is sent to
# this host - a third party you have no agreement with. Nothing identifying the diver goes
# with it (no account, dive, token; the source IP is your server's), and caching means it
# happens once per place rather than once per page view. But if you publish a privacy
# policy, this belongs in it.
#
# Must be a Nominatim-compatible host - that's the wire format the client speaks. Point it
# at your own Nominatim to keep the data in-house, or set it to "" to switch geocoding off
# entirely; the endpoints then answer "no result" instead of failing, which is the same
# thing they do when the provider is unreachable.
# GEOCODER_URL="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org"
# Only for a hosted mirror that wants one (sent as the `key` query parameter). The public
# Nominatim instance is keyless.
# GEOCODER_API_KEY=""
#
# Language the provider answers in, and it is asked for explicitly because the alternative
# is not "no preference" - it's the local script. Unasked, the Blue Hole reverse-geocodes
# to "دهب, مصر", which is then what lands in the dive site's location. One value for the
# whole instance, not per caller: it's part of the cache key.
# GEOCODER_LANGUAGE="en"
#
# Nominatim's usage policy REQUIRES a User-Agent that identifies the application and
# blocks generic ones. If you run a public instance of your own, put your own project and
# contact address here - it is where the provider's operators will get in touch before
# they block you.
# GEOCODER_USER_AGENT="OpenDiving (+https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving-api)"
#
# The rest of that policy is caching (every answer is held in Redis - a month for a hit,
# an hour for a miss) and a cap of one request per second, which the two limits below
# enforce. They behave differently on purpose. The per-user one 429s the account that spent
# it. The provider one is counted across everybody, so hitting it skips the call and
# answers "no suggestion" rather than rejecting whoever happened to arrive second.
# Running your own Nominatim? Raise the provider limit - it's their cap, not yours.
# GEOCODER_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# GEOCODER_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=600
# GEOCODER_PROVIDER_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=1
# GEOCODER_PROVIDER_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=1
#
# "No suggestion" there means exactly that, and it is a contract rather than an implementation
# detail: /geocode/reverse answers 200 with a null body whenever this instance could not ask -
# over the provider cap, provider unreachable, or geocoding switched off - and 204 only when the
# provider was asked and the position has no name. Clients clear a typed-in location on the 204
# and leave it alone on the null, so setting these too low costs suggestions, never data.
#
# Whatever provider you point this at, its attribution travels with each result and the
# clients are expected to show it. For OpenStreetMap data that is a licence condition.
#
# One exception to all of the above: a pin in genuinely open water is named ("Red Sea")
# from public-domain polygons carried in this repo, with no provider involved - no request
# leaves, no key is needed and no rate limit applies. Nominatim has no row for such a
# point, which is why it is done locally. It is a fallback only: coastal water falls inside
# an admin boundary and still gets the better answer ("Bali, Indonesia", not "Bali Sea").
# Setting GEOCODER_URL="" switches this off along with everything else - disabled means
# disabled, not "the offshore half still runs".

# -------- Species catalog (GET /api/v1/species/*) --------
# The "Species spotted" picker searches two public registers live and stores each species
# locally the first time a diver picks it, so the catalog fills itself one sighting at a
# time. No account, no key, no bulk import - WoRMS's full-database download is proprietary
# and its webservice asks not to be harvested, so this app only ever asks about one taxon.
#
# PRIVACY: on a cache miss the typed search string is sent to both hosts below - third
# parties you have no agreement with. Nothing identifying the diver goes with it (no
# account, dive or token; the source IP is your server's), and answers are cached for a
# month. If you publish a privacy policy, this belongs in it alongside the geocoder.
#
# WoRMS is the taxonomic authority - scientific names, synonyms, and the accepted-taxon
# mapping every catalog row is keyed on.
# WORMS_API_URL="https://www.marinespecies.org/rest"
# Wikidata (CC0) supplies common names and aliases, matched to WoRMS through property P850.
# It is not optional garnish: WoRMS records exactly one vernacular for the clownfish, in
# Japanese, so without this a diver typing "clownfish" finds nothing.
# WIKIDATA_API_URL="https://www.wikidata.org/w/api.php"
#
# Wikimedia REQUIRES a User-Agent that identifies the application and blocks generic ones;
# WoRMS asks to be told who is calling. One string for both. Running a public instance of
# your own? Put your own project and contact address here.
# SPECIES_USER_AGENT="OpenDiving (+https://github.com/opendiving/opendiving-api)"
#
# Two tiers of limit, behaving differently on purpose - the same split the geocoder uses.
# The per-user one is shared by search and resolve and 429s the account that spent it. The
# per-provider ones are counted across everybody, so hitting one drops that provider out of
# the search rather than rejecting whoever arrived second; the other still answers.
# SPECIES_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# SPECIES_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=600
# SPECIES_WORMS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# SPECIES_WORMS_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120
# SPECIES_WIKIDATA_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# SPECIES_WIKIDATA_RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=300
#
# Search degrades and never fails: with both hosts unreachable it still answers from the
# local catalog, so a species anyone has ever logged stays findable. Resolving a species
# that is *not* yet local is the one thing that cannot degrade - it 503s, because inventing
# a catalog row without the authoritative record is worse than asking the diver to retry.

# -------- Google Sign-In (optional) --------
# Both halves of one OAuth 2.0 client from the Google Cloud Console, "Web application"
# type. Set both or neither: with an id and no secret the API refuses to start, naming
# them, because signing in with Google redeems an authorization code at Google's token
# endpoint and that takes the secret.
#
# The id is not a secret - it travels in a URL the visitor can read, and it must match the
# frontend's NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. The secret is one, and stays on the API: it is
# never sent to the browser and never given to the web container.
#
# Register {FRONTEND_URL}/auth/google/callback as an Authorized redirect URI on that same
# client, or every sign-in comes back 401. Nothing of Google's runs in the browser: the
# front and sign-in pages contact Google not at all, and pressing the button navigates out
# to Google. See "Setting up Google sign-in" and "Third-party calls" in
# docs/self-hosting/configuration.md.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your-client-secret"

# -------- Magic-link sign-in --------
# MAGIC_LINK_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=900
# MAGIC_LINK_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_EMAIL=3
# MAGIC_LINK_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=15
# MAGIC_LINK_VERIFY_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=30
# Wrong guesses allowed against the six-digit code the sign-in email prints beside the
# link. Running out spends the code and nothing else - the link in the same email keeps
# working, which is what stops a burnt code from becoming a way to lock someone out.
# SIGN_IN_CODE_ATTEMPTS_MAX=5
# EMAIL_CHANGE_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
# EMAIL_CHANGE_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=3

# -------- Passkeys --------
# Nothing here is required, and there is no on/off switch: the browser's own capability
# detection is the switch, and a server flag would just be a second way for it to be
# wrong. The relying-party id and the expected origin are derived from FRONTEND_URL
# above - which is therefore the passkey domain, and changing its *hostname* orphans
# every passkey already registered (browsers scope credentials by hostname). The magic
# link is the recovery path when that happens.
#
# Browsers only expose WebAuthn in a secure context, so a plain-HTTP instance gets no
# passkeys at all and the UI hides itself. `localhost` counts as secure; a bare IP
# address never does, certificate or not.
#
# How long a started ceremony stays completable. It is consumed on the first verify
# attempt regardless, so this only bounds an abandoned one.
# PASSKEY_CHALLENGE_TTL_SECONDS=600
# PASSKEY_MAX_CREDENTIALS_PER_USER=10
# Per-IP over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. The options ceiling is high because
# one is minted per signed-out page view that supports autofill, and an office behind a
# single NAT gateway is one IP here.
# PASSKEY_OPTIONS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=240
# PASSKEY_VERIFY_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IP=30
# PASSKEY_REGISTER_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- Full export (GET /api/v1/export/*) --------
# Fixed-window rate limit, keyed per user and **shared across all three export endpoints**
# - the budget bounds total export work, so ten CSV downloads do use up the archive's
# allowance too. Authenticated and owner-only, so this is not an abuse boundary the way
# the contact form's is; it's here because one archive request reads every dive-computer
# file and c-card image the caller owns. Generous on purpose: this is a button a diver
# presses once, and a nightly backup of your own account should not hit it.
# EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# EXPORT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=10

# -------- Gear service reminders --------
# Hour of day (UTC) the "your gear is due for service" digest cron runs.
# GEAR_SERVICE_DIGEST_HOUR=7

# -------- Account deletion --------
# How long a deleted account stays restorable before the hourly purge destroys it and
# everything it owns - dives, sites, certifications, gear, and the uploaded files behind
# them. The account is dark from the moment the user presses the button either way; this
# is only how long the way back in stays open.
#
# 0 means the next sweep purges it, with no way back. The confirmation email says so
# rather than telling the user to sign in before a date that has already passed.
#
# The bundled privacy page promises erasure "within 30 days", so raising this past about
# 29 makes that sentence false for your instance - see docs/self-hosting/configuration.md.
# ACCOUNT_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=14
# Per-user over MAGIC_LINK_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS. DELETE /user sends mail; this bounds
# that, and is not what stops a double submit (the endpoint's database predicate is).
# ACCOUNT_DELETION_RATE_LIMIT_PER_USER=5

# -------- Admin interface (CRUDAdmin, mounted at /admin) --------
# OFF, and this whole block is commented out on purpose. The panel talks to the models
# directly, bypassing every ownership check in api/v1, so it is a deliberate opt-in rather
# than something a fresh install inherits. Enabling it means uncommenting CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED
# here AND setting a real ADMIN_PASSWORD above; in production, startup refuses without one.
# Restrict who can reach it while you're at it - comma-separated, both optional:
# CRUD_ADMIN_ENABLED=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS="203.0.113.7,203.0.113.8"
# CRUD_ADMIN_ALLOWED_NETWORKS="10.0.0.0/8"
# CRUD_ADMIN_MOUNT_PATH="/admin"
# CRUD_ADMIN_MAX_SESSIONS=10
# CRUD_ADMIN_SESSION_TIMEOUT=1440
#
# ---- Running the panel with more than one worker ----
# The shipped image runs `gunicorn -w 4`. One setting is required for that, and one
# existing default must not be turned off.
#
# REQUIRED - CRUD_ADMIN_DB_URL: where the panel's own tables live. Unset means a SQLite
# file inside a single container, which every worker races to create and no other
# container can read. Point it at the app's Postgres instead (note the +asyncpg driver):
# CRUD_ADMIN_DB_URL="postgresql+asyncpg://postgres:your-postgres-password@db:5432/opendive"
#
# SESSIONS: admin sessions need a store shared by all workers, or a login handled by one
# worker is not recognised by the next request landing on another and the panel logs you
# straight back out. CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_SESSIONS=true (the default, below) already provides
# this by persisting sessions to the admin_session table, so there is nothing to do -
# just don't set it to false on a multi-worker deployment. CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_ENABLED=true
# is an alternative shared store, useful to keep session lookups off Postgres, but it is
# not required.
#
# Measured with 4 workers: Redis sessions 12/12 requests authenticated, DB-tracked
# sessions 12/12, and both disabled 1/12 - the one request that happened to land on the
# worker holding the session.
#
# Leave all of this unset and the panel works fine on a single worker, which is what
# local development runs via the --reload command in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Session cookies are Secure by default, so they will not survive plain HTTP. Set this
# to false only for local development over http://localhost.
# SESSION_SECURE_COOKIES=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_EVENTS=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_TRACK_SESSIONS=true
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_ENABLED=false
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_HOST="localhost"
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_PORT=6379
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_DB=0
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_PASSWORD=""
# CRUD_ADMIN_REDIS_SSL=false
